"executive_summary": "This study addresses the challenge of designing heterogeneous catalysts for the selective oxidation of propylene, a process complicated by vast materials spaces and complex reaction networks. The authors employ a high-throughput experimentation (HTE) approach to synthesize and test 120 three-component catalysts based on ruthenium, tungsten, and phosphorus supported on SiO2. These experimental results are combined with theoretical descriptors derived from Density Functional Theory (DFT) calculations of free-atom properties. The core methodology utilizes Subgroup Discovery (SGD), an artificial intelligence technique designed to identify local patterns of exceptional performance rather than global averages. By maximizing a quality function based on the Janson-Shannon divergence, the SGD algorithm extracts specific constraints on physicochemical parameters that correlate with high yields of value-added oxygenates (acrolein and acrylic acid). The analysis identifies temperature, phosphorus content, and composition-weighted electronegativity as the critical parameters governing this high performance. These derived 'SG rules' successfully guide the design of more complex four- and five-component catalysts, predicting materials with oxygenate yields up to 60%, which is significantly higher than the maximum yield observed in the training dataset. The study demonstrates that this data-driven approach can uncover interpretable, chemically meaningful rules that rationalize local reactivity patterns and accelerate the discovery of superior catalysts without requiring explicit atomistic modeling of the full catalytic progression.",
